Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Thanks — I can help you shape the finance narrative and give actionable margin + scalability levers. A couple quick clarifying points before I build any numbers or slides:

- You listed: cash $2.1M, burn $240K/month, and runway 14 months. Those three don’t align arithmetically (2.1M / 240K ≈ 8.8 months). Does the 14‑month runway assume revenue growth, an imminent cost-cutting plan, or a different definition of “burn” (e.g., operating cash flow after expected revenue)?  
- To build precise scenarios I’ll need a few items (if you have them): current COGS and gross margin %, monthly/seasonal revenue profile, SG&A broken into payroll/marketing/other, any planned hires or capex, and your fundraising / board timing and target runway.

Below I’ve prepared a concise, practical framework you can use immediately (and that I can expand into slides/models once you confirm the assumptions).

1) Quick reconciliation & core formula
- Runway (months) = Cash on hand / Net cash burn per month.  
- If burn = $240K/month and cash = $2.1M, runway = ~8.8 months. If you want 14 months of runway with $2.1M cash, net burn must be ~$150K/month (2.1M / 14 = 150K). So the immediate near-term objective is to get monthly net burn down from 240K → ~150K (or extend cash via financing).

2) High-impact margin-improvement levers (prioritized by speed-to-impact)
A. Quick wins (30–90 days)
- Freeze non-essential hiring and discretionary spend (marketing, travel, consultants). Expected impact: immediate monthly SG&A reduction.  
- Tactical price increases (1–3%) where customer elasticity allows — low friction, high ROI. Impact example: 1% lift on $9.6M ARR ≈ $96K/year.  
- Negotiate supplier/payment terms (price, volume discounts, extended payables). Can reduce COGS or improve working capital quickly.  
- Reduce product complexity / SKU rationalization — stop low-margin SKUs to improve average gross margin and lower inventory/carrying costs.

B. Operational fixes (60–180 days)
- Improve manufacturing yields, reduce scrap and rework (process audits, quality focus). Improves gross margin on every unit.  
- Shift production to higher-utilization lines or outsource low-utilization lines to reduce fixed cost per unit.  
- Rework sales incentives to favor higher-margin products; tighten discount approval process.

C. Medium-term investments (3–12 months)
- Automate key manual steps on the shop floor (ROI analysis required). Upfront capex but drives unit-cost reductions and scale.  
- Product redesign for manufacturability (reduce BOM cost, simplify assembly) to permanently raise gross margins.  
- Introduce higher-margin services (warranty, maintenance contracts, spare parts, consumables).

3) Scalability levers (to improve margins as you grow)
- Standardize product platforms to gain volume leverage and lower per-unit COGS.  
- Centralize purchasing and use aggregated volumes to negotiate better supplier pricing.  
- Use configurable modules vs fully custom builds to reduce engineering hours per sale.  
- Implement KPI-driven production scheduling to reach higher utilization and reduce fixed-cost dilution.  
- Data-driven pricing (segment customers by price sensitivity and value capture).

4) Financial impact — illustrative scenarios (using your revenue note)
Assumptions used for examples: Quarterly revenue = $2.4M → monthly revenue ≈ $800K, annualized revenue ≈ $9.6M. These are examples — I can replace them with your actuals.

- Scenario A — Base (no change): net burn = $240K/month → runway ≈ 8.8 months.
- Scenario B — Quick cuts + 1% price lift: assume combined reduces net burn by $60K/month → new burn = $180K → runway = 11.7 months.
- Scenario C — Aggressive mix (1.5% price lift + 5% COGS reduction + 15% SG&A cut): approximate net burn reduction ~150K/month → new burn = 90K → runway ≈ 23.3 months.

(Those reductions are illustrative; I’ll build precise impact estimates once you share COGS and SG&A splits.)

5) Suggested internal finance narrative (one page / board slide)
- Current position: cash $2.1M, revenue run-rate $9.6M, current reported net cash burn $240K/month (runway X months).  
- Key drivers: margin pressure from [list causes you know: raw-material inflation, low utilization, discounting], planned investments/hiring, fundraising timeline.  
- Risk: at current pace runway insufficient for strategic options (M&A/growth, product investments, or reaching next revenue milestone).  
- Plan (30/90/180 days): list top 6 levers (freeze hiring, price adjustments, supplier renegotiation, SKU rationalization, efficiency push, targeted automation). For each, include owner, target $ impact, and timing.  
- Financial scenarios: conservative / base / aggressive — projected cash runway and EBITDA profile.  
- Ask: approve X-month hiring freeze, approval to pursue 1–2 strategic supplier renegotiations, and permission to present a detailed scenario model in 2 weeks.

6) Next steps I can do for you
- Build a 3-scenario cash / P&L model (base, improvement, aggressive) that shows monthly runway and the dollar impact of each lever — I’ll need: COGS, gross margin %, SG&A broken down by category, monthly revenue, and planned hires/capex.  
- Draft the one-page finance narrative/board slide with the bullets above and a one-page action plan with owners and timelines.  
- Run sensitivity analyses (price, COGS, SG&A) so you can show the board how much each lever buys in months of runway.

Which do you want me to prepare next — (A) a 3-scenario cash/P&L model (I’ll need the detailed inputs), or (B) the one-page finance narrative + slide deck (I can draft with high-level assumptions and you can iterate)? Also confirm whether the 14‑month runway already includes any of the cuts or revenue expectations so I use consistent assumptions.

