Below are recommended pieces for the finance narrative, prioritized margin-improvement levers (with likely impact and time-to-implement), a short scenario framework, and next steps I can take for you.

Executive finance narrative (structure)
- Current snapshot (single slide): cash, runway, burn, revenue last quarter, revenue run-rate, gross margin, EBITDA burn. Call out any customer concentration or one-time items.
- Core challenge & objective (one sentence): e.g., “Extend runway to >18 months and drive operating leverage to reach cash-neutral operations within 12 months while protecting growth.”
- Key levers to improve margins (bulleted, prioritized).
- 90/180/365-day plan with KPIs and owners.
- Ask / decisions needed (hiring freeze, approval to renegotiate supplier contracts, fundraising target, etc.)

Top levers to improve margin and scalability (prioritized)
Quick wins (0–90 days — low complexity, high ROI)
- Pricing & discount discipline: Stop or limit promotional discounts, revise contract terms, introduce minimum contract length. Impact: immediate lift to revenue per sale; often improves margin by 2–5%+ of revenue.
- Tighten procurement & supplier negotiations: renegotiate rates, payment terms, volume discounts. Impact: direct reduction in COGS; could save tens of thousands per month quickly.
- Reduce non-essential discretionary spend: travel, consultants, marketing channels with poor ROI. Impact: immediate burn reduction.
- Credit and collections: shorten DSO, tighten payment terms. Improves cash; small effect on P&L but increases available runway.

Medium-term actions (90–180 days — moderate complexity)
- Shift sales mix to higher-margin products/services: emphasize upsell/cross-sell and product bundles. Impact: incremental margin lift and better unit economics.
- Revisit pricing tiers and packaging: add value tiers or usage pricing to capture more customer surplus.
- Efficiency in customer acquisition (optimize channels, improve conversion, focus on high-LTV segments). Impact: reduces CAC and shortens payback.
- Headcount optimization: hiring freeze for non-critical roles, restructure teams for productivity. Impact: material reduction in fixed costs if implemented carefully.

Longer-term / strategic (180+ days)
- Product engineering to reduce COGS: re-architecture or automation to lower cost-to-serve. Impact: sustainable gross margin improvement.
- Move to higher-margin revenue models (SaaS subscriptions, licensing, maintenance). Impact: recurring revenue improves predictability and valuation.
- Strategic partnerships and channels to scale with lower sales expense.
- Automation & tooling to reduce manual operating expense (billing, support, ops).

Impact examples (illustrative — will customize once you confirm which burn number is correct)
Assume current monthly revenue = $800K (derived from $2.4M last quarter), cash = $2.1M.

Example 1 — reduce COGS by 5% of revenue
- Savings = 5% * $800K = $40K/month
- If net burn = $240K/month, new burn ≈ $200K → runway 2.1 / 0.20 = 10.5 months (gain ~1.7 months).
- If actual burn is $150K/month (14-month stated runway), savings extend runway noticeably further.

Example 2 — combined savings + 10% revenue growth / quarter (compounded)
- Revenue growth increases gross margin dollars and may reduce net burn faster; combined with 5% COGS cut you accelerate path to cash-neutral.

Example 3 — headcount / G&A cut of $60K/month
- Direct runway extension: 2.1 / (burn - 60K). If burn 240K → new burn 180K → runway = 11.7 months.

(Exact outcomes depend on whether burn is pre- or post-revenue; I’ll run precise sensitivity tables once you confirm the burn/runway definitions and margins.)

