Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Thanks — this is a great starting point. Before I build slides or a model, one quick data clarification so I don’t build on the wrong assumption:

- When you say “burn rate $240K/month” is that net burn (cash outflows minus cash inflows) or gross burn (total cash outflows before revenue)?  
- You say “14 months runway” with $2.1M cash — by the simple cash / burn formula that implies a burn of ~$150K/month (2.1M / 14 ≈ 150K). If burn is actually $240K/month, runway is ~8.8 months (2.1M / 240K). Which is correct, or does the 14-month figure already factor in forecasted revenue/inflows?

If you confirm those, I’ll produce a model and slides. In the meantime, here are immediate diagnostics, prioritized margin-improvement & scalability levers, a short finance narrative you can reuse, a 30/60/90 plan, and recommended KPIs.

Immediate diagnostics (what I’d check now)
- Verify burn definition (net vs gross) and which month(s) were used to calculate it.  
- P&L detail for last 6 months: revenue by product/customer, COGS (materials, labor, freight), SG&A (S&M, R&D, G&A), one-time items.  
- Working capital and AR/AP days, backlog, customer concentration, price tiers and discounts, contract terms.  
- Capacity utilization and any near-term capital spend commitments.  

High-level financial math (to align expectations)
- If burn = 240K/month and cash = 2.1M → runway = 2.1M / 240K ≈ 8.75 months.  
- If runway = 14 months and cash = 2.1M → implied net burn ≈ 150K/month.  
- If revenue last quarter = 2.4M → average monthly revenue ≈ 800K. We need margins to translate that revenue into cash flow; revenue alone doesn’t define runway.

Priority margin-improvement levers (ranked by typical speed-to-impact)
1. Pricing & commercial (fast, high impact)
   - Reprice where possible (targeted 3–7% price increases on legacy accounts; more for low-value discount channels).  
   - Move customers to value-based pricing/volume discounts that protect margin.  
   - Reduce or eliminate low-margin deals and channels; institute minimum margins/price floors.

2. Product / portfolio (high impact, moderate speed)
   - SKU rationalization: stop low-volume, low-margin SKUs (reduces COGS complexity, inventory).  
   - Shift mix to higher-margin products or aftermarket/recurring revenue (service, parts, contracts).  
   - Standardize components to reduce procurement costs.

3. Procurement & COGS (moderate speed, high impact)
   - Renegotiate supplier contracts, consolidate vendors, lock in volume pricing.  
   - Substitute materials where feasible, optimize BOMs, and reduce scrap/waste.  
   - Improve yield / manufacturing throughput (reduce direct labor & rework).

4. Operational efficiency (moderate speed)
   - Improve capacity utilization and scheduling to reduce per-unit fixed cost.  
   - Invest selectively in automation where ROI is <18 months.  
   - Tighten quality control to lower rework and returns.

5. SG&A & discretionary spend (fast to implement)
   - Freeze nonessential hiring, review contractor spend, and reduce travel/events.  
   - Reprioritize R&D to highest ROI projects; defer long-lead investments.  

6. Working capital optimization (fast)
   - Shorten AR days (incentives for faster payment), extend AP terms where possible, reduce inventory days.

7. Revenue acceleration (parallel with cuts)
   - Sales enablement for higher-ticket items, upsell/cross-sell campaigns, prioritize leads with short sales cycles.  
   - OEM or channel partnerships to scale distribution with less fixed cost.

Estimated impact ranges (typical order-of-magnitude)
- Price increases: +2–7% on revenue → direct margin lift depending on cost base.  
- Procurement/COGS improvements: 5–15% reduction in COGS for targeted SKUs.  
- SKU rationalization & efficiency: 3–8% margin uplift.  
- SG&A reduction: 10–30% of discretionary spend can often be removed with limited revenue impact.

Scalability levers (to make growth less cash-intensive)
- Product modularization: reduce engineering complexity and speed new product introductions.  
- Platform approach: reuse common subassemblies across more SKUs to lower incremental cost.  
- Shift revenue mix toward recurring services/parts and extended warranties.  
- Outsource non-core manufacturing to variable-cost suppliers (turn fixed costs into variable).  
- Digitalize sales (e-commerce/order portals) to reduce SG&A per order and increase conversion.

Finance narrative (two-paragraph executive-ready version)
- “Metro Manufacturing begins from a position of solid top-line traction (Q4 revenue run‑rate: $2.4M last quarter, ≈$800K/month) and $2.1M in cash. Our immediate focus is to protect runway while we scale margin-accretive growth: we will reconcile burn definitions and confirm runway assumptions, then implement a prioritized set of pricing, product‑mix and cost actions to reduce net burn and improve gross margin. Our target over the next 90 days is to reduce net burn by at least 30% through targeted pricing changes, SKU rationalization, and procurement savings, preserving optionality for growth.”  
- “By month six we intend to convert early operational improvements into durable margin expansion and capacity to deploy efficient growth capital. We will present scenario-based forecasts (base, downside, upside) that show required funding or break-even points, and tie each scenario to concrete milestones (supplier rebates, price implementation, headcount plan) so the board can evaluate trade-offs clearly.”

30 / 60 / 90 day tactical plan (prioritized)
- Days 0–30 (stabilize)
  - Confirm burn/runway math and produce a 3-month cash forecast.  
  - Freeze discretionary hiring and noncritical CapEx.  
  - Quick-win pricing changes for 10–20% of customer base; start supplier renegotiations.  
  - Start AR collection push and extend AP days 10–30 days where feasible.
- Days 31–60 (execute)
  - Implement SKU rationalization plan; accelerate higher-margin products.  
  - Finalize supplier renegotiations and initial procurement consolidations.  
  - Launch 2–3 targeted sales initiatives to accelerate cash sales; quantify expected lift.  
  - Deliver updated monthly reporting (revenue by product, gross margin by product).
- Days 61–90 (lock in & scale)
  - Review progress against burn reduction targets; adjust course.  
  - If burn target met, evaluate selective re-investment (sales expansion, high-ROI automation).  
  - Prepare board materials with scenario analysis and funding ask (if needed).

KPIs to include in internal materials
- Cash balance and runway (explicitly state assumptions).  
- Net cash burn (monthly) and trend.  
- Gross margin and gross profit dollars by product line.  
- Contribution margin per product/customer.  
- Revenue growth rate (MoM/YoY) and booking velocity.  
- AR days, AP days, inventory days (working capital).  
- Customer concentration (top 5 customers % of revenue).  
- Capacity utilization and yield metrics.

Suggested slide deck structure (5–7 slides)
1. Executive summary: current cash, runway (with assumptions), last quarter revenue, headline ask and goals.  
2. Where we are: P&L snapshot last quarter, burn drivers, major one-offs.  
3. Scenarios: base / downside / upside — cash runway and funding needs for each.  
4. Levers to improve margin & burn: prioritized list with expected timing and impact.  
5. 90-day plan and milestones (what will be measured weekly).  
6. KPIs and reporting cadence.  
7. Ask / decision needed (e.g., approve restructuring, approve pricing changes, authorize fundraising prep).

Example financial target you can present (illustrative)
- Goal: reduce net burn from $240K → $150K/mo in 90 days (saves $90K/mo). Ways to achieve:
  - Price & commercial: +$30K/mo contribution (through price/terms improvements).  
  - Procurement/COGS: +$25K/mo (renegotiation & BOM simplification).  
  - SG&A cuts & working capital: +$35K/mo.  
  - These are illustrative — I’ll build a detailed P&L opportunity model if you want.

Next steps I can take for you
- Draft the 5–7 slide internal deck and the executive finance narrative using your confirmed burn/runway assumption (I’ll include the math and scenario table).  
- Or build a one-page operating model (monthly for 12 months) using your P&L line items so we can test sensitivity of runway to price, margin, and cuts.

Tell me:
1) Which burn/runway definition is correct (or provide last 3 months cash flow / P&L), and  
2) Which deliverable you want first: slide deck, 12-month model, or both — and I’ll produce it.

