A. High‑impact levers (what to do, typical timing, expected monthly savings / margin uplift)
1) Improve gross margin (highest leverage if manufacturing)
   - Renegotiate supplier contracts and volume discounts (5–15% COGS reduction; 30–90 days to realize).
   - Material substitution / design for manufacturability to lower BOM cost (3–9 months).
   - Improve yields / reduce scrap via process controls & quality initiatives (2–6 months).
   - SKU rationalization — drop low-margin SKUs and focus capacity on high‑margin SKUs (30–90 days).
   - Example impact: on $800k/month revenue (2.4M last quarter → ~800k/mo), a 5 p.p. gross margin increase = 800k × 5% = $40k/month; a 10 p.p. improvement = $80k/month.

2) Reduce operating expense (quick runway benefit)
   - Hiring freeze + pause noncritical spend (immediate).
   - Trim discretionary spend (travel, consultants, contractors) (immediate → 30 days).
   - Consolidate G&A roles, renegotiate SaaS & leases (30–90 days).
   - Example impact: if monthly OpEx is ~1.04M (revenue 800k + burn 240k → expenses ≈ 1.04M), a 10% OpEx cut ≈ $104k/month.

3) Improve working capital (extend runway without equity)
   - Shorten DSO: tighten invoicing, offer small discounts for early payment (30–60 days).  
   - Extend DPO: renegotiate supplier payment terms (30–90 days).
   - Reduce inventory days via JIT or better forecasting; consider vendor-managed inventory (60–120 days).
   - Use receivable financing or factoring as a bridge if cash is tight (immediate).
   - Impact: freeing 1–2 months of working capital can be equivalent to large one‑time cash inflow.

4) Revenue and product strategy (sustainable)
   - Reprice where value supports it (even +2–5% price can meaningfully add margin).
   - Shift sales to higher-margin channels or customers (focus on products with >target margin).
   - Add aftermarket/service, maintenance, or consumables with higher margins.
   - Impact: margin expansion and better recurring revenue mix (3–9 months to ramp).

5) Scalability & capital choices
   - Outsource / contract manufacturing vs. CAPEX for automation — build decision on unit economics and utilization.
   - Modular production lines to scale incrementally (reduces upfront capex).
   - Prepare clear ROI for automation investments (payback < 24 months preferable).
   - Financing options: revenue-based financing, short-term bridge, or structured debt on receivables/inventory if equity dilution is undesirable.

B. Scenario examples (using numbers to show how to get to 14 months)
Assumptions you gave: cash = $2.1M, revenue ≈ $800k/month (2.4M / 3), burn = $240k/month (net cash outflow).

