Nice — I can help. Below I’ll (1) flag one important data check, (2) show a concise baseline and three illustrative improvement scenarios with numbers so you can see the impact on burn and runway, (3) list prioritized margin & scalability levers you can present to the team/board, (4) give a short ready-to-use finance narrative and slide outline, and (5) recommend next steps and what data I need to build a crisp model or slides.

1) Quick data check (important)
- You said cash = $2.1M, burn = $240K/month, and runway = 14 months. Simple cash/runway = 2.1M / 240K ≈ 8.8 months. So your 14‑month runway must be using projected revenues or planned cost reductions. Please confirm how you’re calculating runway (cash-only vs. cash + forecasted net inflows) so I can ensure the narrative is internally consistent.

2) Baseline (illustrative) and scenarios
- Facts you gave: revenue last quarter = $2.4M → monthly revenue ≈ $800K.
- We don’t have COGS or OpEx split. I’ll use one plausible baseline that matches your stated burn (used only for illustration; I’ll update if you share actuals).

Illustrative baseline (to match burn = $240K):
- Monthly revenue = $800K
- Assume gross margin = 30% → gross profit = $240K
- Operating expenses (SG&A + other non-COGS opex) = $480K
- Net cash burn = 480K - 240K = $240K → runway = 2.1M / 240K = 8.8 months

Scenario outcomes (based on simple monthly P&L changes):

A) Conservative improvement (low risk)
- GM ↑ from 30% → 33% (+3pp) → gross profit = $264K (+$24K)
- OpEx ↓ 10% → $432K (-$48K)
- New burn = 432K - 264K = $168K → runway ≈ 12.5 months

B) Realistic achievable (mix of pricing, procurement, efficiency)
- GM ↑ to 36% (+6pp) → gross profit = $288K (+$48K)
- OpEx ↓ 10% → $432K (-$48K)
- New burn = 432K - 288K = $144K → runway ≈ 14.6 months

C) Aggressive / high-impact (combination of price, SKU rationalization, automation)
- GM ↑ to 40% (+10pp) → gross profit = $320K (+$80K)
- OpEx ↓ 15% → $408K (-$72K)
- New burn = 408K - 320K = $88K → runway ≈ 23.9 months

Takeaway: relatively modest gross margin and OpEx improvements (3–6pp GM + 10% OpEx cuts) move you from ~9 months to ~12–15 months runway. Bigger combined moves dramatically extend runway.

3) Priority levers (organized by speed-to-impact)
Immediate / 30 days (high impact, low implementation time)
- Pricing: targeted price increases on top SKUs, short A/B tests on new orders; focus on customers/products with inelastic demand.
- SKU/product mix: pause low-volume, low-margin SKUs; promote higher-margin SKUs.
- Supplier renegotiation: top 3–5 suppliers for better pricing, payment terms, or volume discounts.
- Freeze discretionary spend / hiring freeze; prioritize critical roles only.
- Working capital: tighten DSO, extend DPO (where relationships allow), reduce inventory buffers (safety stock review).

30–90 days (medium-term)
- Manufacturing productivity: quick wins in throughput and yield (reduce scrap/rework); line balancing; reduce changeover time (SMED).
- Lean & OEE initiatives: establish OEE baseline, target top losses.
- Outsource vs. insource review: evaluate contract manufacturing for non-core SKUs to defer CapEx and convert fixed costs to variable.
- Warranty/returns reduction: root-cause analysis to cut product failures.

90–180 days (investments with ROI)
- Automation / CAPEX with clear payback: robotic cells, inline testing if payback < 18–24 months.
- Product redesign for cost reduction (DFM), BOM optimization and alternate components.
- New higher-margin services or aftermarket programs (spare parts, maintenance contracts).

4) Scalability considerations (how margin interacts with scale)
- Fixed vs variable costs: identify which costs scale with volume. If fixed costs are high, revenue growth will leverage gross margin into EBITDA quickly.
- Unit economics: define contribution margin per unit; target customer/product segments with best unit contribution.
- Capacity constraints: identify bottlenecks and incremental cost to serve (is next 10% revenue incremental margin positive or negative?).
- Invest vs rent: prefer flexible capacity (CMO/contract mfg) until demand visibility improves.
- Working capital scaling: growth increases inventory and receivables — plan financing or supplier/payable terms.

5) KPIs to include on slides/dashboard
- Runway (cash-only) and runway (forecasted) with assumptions
- Monthly revenue, QoQ growth, and revenue by product/segment
- Gross margin % and contribution margin per product
- Opex broken down by fixed vs variable; burn rate
- Inventory turns, DSO, DPO, net working capital days
- OEE, scrap rate, yield, capacity utilization
- Customer / product profitability (top 20 customers / SKUs)

6) Short finance narrative (use in slide or opening remarks)
One-paragraph executive summary:
"Metro Manufacturing finished last quarter with $2.4M in revenue; current cash on hand is $2.1M. At our existing cost and margin structure, our simple cash-only runway is under 9 months (we currently model ~14 months when including projected revenues and planned cost actions). To secure growth and optionality, we recommend a focused three-tier plan: (1) immediate margin recovery via targeted pricing and SKU rationalization, supplier and working‑capital negotiations, and a temporary reduction in discretionary spend; (2) 30–90 day operational efficiency programs in manufacturing (OEE, yield, changeover), and (3) 90–180 day investments in automation and product redesign where ROI < 24 months. Combined, these moves materially extend runway and improve EBITDA leverage as revenue scales."

Two-sentence board ask:
"Approve an immediate implementation package to capture 6–8pp gross margin and 10–15% OpEx savings across high-impact areas over the next 90 days and authorize a short-term working capital/financing contingency plan if growth requires capacity investment. We will present a stress-tested 3-scenario P&L with KPIs and a recommended financing timeline at the next board meeting."

7) Slide deck outline (6–8 slides)
- Slide 1: Executive summary + board ask (one slide)
- Slide 2: Current financials & runway assumptions (cash-only and forecasted)
- Slide 3: Key levers and expected impact (quantified scenarios)
- Slide 4: Operational plan & timeline (30/60/90 day milestones)
- Slide 5: KPIs and dashboard (what we will track weekly/monthly)
- Slide 6: Risk & mitigation + financing options
- Slide 7: Appendix — P&L assumptions and sensitivity (3 scenarios)

8) Next steps / what I need from you to prepare a model or slides
Please share:
- Actual monthly P&L (last 6 months): revenue, COGS, SG&A breakdown
- Top 20 SKUs by revenue and gross margin (or product family)
- Top 10 suppliers and major contracts (volumes, pricing tiers)
- Inventory, DSO, DPO figures and recent trends
- Any committed CapEx and hiring plans
With those I’ll build a 3-scenario P&L and a slide-ready one-page executive summary you can present to the exec team or board.

If you want, I can draft the one‑page executive slide + 3-scenario P&L now — tell me whether to use the illustrative baseline above or provide the actual P&L.

