Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Thanks — I can help. First, one quick clarification so I use the right baseline numbers:

- When you say “burn rate $240K/month,” is that net burn (cash outflow after operating revenue) or gross burn (total cash outflows before revenue)?  
- The runway you gave (14 months) doesn’t match cash ÷ burn if burn = $240K: 2.1M / 240K = 8.75 months. If runway = 14 months, that implies either a lower net burn or that you’re counting expected revenue and cost reductions in the runway. Which definition are you using?

If you don’t have that ready, I’ll proceed with the common assumption that burn = net cash burn. Below I give: quick calculations, high-impact margin & scalability actions, a prioritized 90-day plan, key KPIs to present, and a short finance narrative you can drop into internal materials. I can then build a one-page slide or a simple scenario model if you want — tell me which assumption you prefer.

Quick math (assumption A — burn = net cash burn = $240K/month)
- Runway = Cash / Burn = $2,100,000 / $240,000 = 8.75 months.
- Last quarter revenue = $2.4M → monthly revenue ≈ $800K.

If instead burn = operating expense (before revenue) we need more detail to estimate net cash flow.

Scenario examples (if burn = net $240K/mo)
- Base: burn = $240K → runway ≈ 8.8 months.
- Moderate cost improvement (20% reduction in burn): burn = $192K → runway ≈ 10.9 months (+2.1 months).
- Aggressive cost improvement (35% reduction): burn = $156K → runway ≈ 13.5 months (+4.7 months).
- Revenue lift scenario: if you grow revenue and reduce net burn by $80K/month (e.g., raise contribution margin), burn = $160K → runway ≈ 13.1 months.

Actionable margin-improvement levers (ranked by speed/impact)
1. Improve gross margins (highest leverage)
   - Price optimization: test targeted price increases for high-demand SKUs; add premium tiers/upsells.
   - Product mix: push higher-margin SKUs; bundle low-margin items into packages with services.
   - Lower COGS: renegotiate supplier contracts, consolidate vendors, source components competitively, vertical integration where sensible.
   - Design for cost: reduce parts complexity, standardize components, reduce BOM costs.
2. Reduce variable & manufacturing costs
   - Improve yields / reduce scrap with root-cause and quality programs (Lean / Six Sigma).
   - Increase capacity utilization / run larger production batches where holding costs allow.
   - Shift some manufacturing to contract manufacturers for lower fixed costs.
3. Optimize working capital
   - Reduce inventory days (JIT, forecast-driven procurement).
   - Tighten receivables: shorten payment terms, early-pay discounts, factoring if needed.
   - Stretch payables selectively without harming supplier relationships.
4. SG&A and overhead
   - Freeze new hires; review and defer non-critical projects.
   - Consolidate offices / reduce discretionary spend (travel, consultancies).
   - Outsource non-core functions (payroll, IT ops).
5. Strategic revenue margin expansion
   - After-sales services, spare parts, consumables (higher margin recurring revenue).
   - Financing, warranty or subscription models where customers will pay for uptime.
   - Channel optimization: shift to higher margin channels or direct sales for key accounts.
6. One-time / structural options
   - Renegotiate leases, restructure debt, consider sale-leaseback of assets.
   - Temporary compensation adjustments, if necessary and acceptable culturally.

Scalability levers for manufacturing (how to grow margins as you scale)
- Automation & capital efficiency: incremental automation that reduces labor per unit as volume rises.
- Modular production cells to add capacity without linear cost increases.
- Standardize product platforms so incremental SKUs reuse existing tooling and supply chains.
- Digitalize operations (ERP / MES) to reduce order-to-cash cycle, improve planning, and reduce expediting costs.
- Build scalable channel partners / distributors so sales growth doesn’t require proportional salesheadcount.
- Customer segmentation: prioritize customers with better payment terms and lower support costs.

90-day prioritized plan (what to show internally and execute fast)
Week 0–2: Diagnostics & stabilization
- Confirm definition of burn and reconcile cash-flow waterfall (cash balance → monthly inflows and outflows).
- Run a top-10 customers & products profitability review.
- Identify 3 “quick wins” for immediate cost reductions (supplier renegotiation, discretionary spend freeze, AR collection push).

Month 1: Quick actions
- Implement 1–2 price increases or upsell campaigns for high-margin SKUs (A/B test with top accounts).
- Launch supplier renegotiation and identify alternative suppliers for high-cost parts.
- Tighten AP/AR terms and accelerate collections.

Month 2–3: Operational improvements
- Start Lean/quality initiatives on highest-loss production lines.
- Implement inventory optimization (reduce slow-moving SKUs, safety stock rationalization).
- Roll out tighter capital spend approvals; postpone non-critical capex.

Month 3–6: Scale and embed
- Begin automation projects with clear IRR > target threshold.
- Launch new services / consumables revenue pilots.
- Implement ERP or analytics for margin-by-SKU tracking.

KPIs to include in the finance narrative or slides
- Cash balance, monthly net burn, runway (months).
- Revenue (quarterly & monthly run-rate) and YoY growth.
- Gross margin % by product family and consolidated.
- Contribution margin per product/customer.
- EBITDA / Adjusted EBITDA.
- AR days, inventory days, AP days, cash conversion cycle.
- Capacity utilization, scrap rate, on-time delivery.
- Customer concentration (revenue % of top 3/5 customers).
- Forecast vs. actual cash flow for next 12 months (scenario bands).

Example short finance narrative (Executive Summary you can paste)
- “Metro Manufacturing enters this quarter with $2.1M cash. Based on our current reported net cash burn of $240k/month, the company has roughly 8.8 months of runway. We generated $2.4M in revenue last quarter (≈$800k monthly). We have clear levers to extend runway and materially improve margins: (1) gross-margin improvement through pricing and COGS reduction, (2) working-capital optimization, and (3) targeted automation and product mix changes to scale margins as volumes grow. Our 90‑day plan focuses on immediate margin capture and cash preservation (supplier renegotiations, AR push, discretionary spend freeze), followed by operational changes (Lean, inventory rationalization) that unlock multi-month runway extension. We expect conservative execution of the plan to improve monthly net burn by $60–120k within 90 days and 20–35% over 6–12 months; we’ll present modeled scenarios and KPIs to the Board for approval next week.”

Template slide / section headings for internal materials
1. Current position (cash, burn, runway, revenue run-rate, gross margin)
2. Key issues & risks (customer concentration, inventory, supplier risk)
3. Proposed actions (90-day quick wins + 3–12 month initiatives)
4. Expected impact (scenario table: base / cost-only / revenue-only / combined)
5. KPIs & cadence (who owns each metric, reporting cadence)
6. Asks / decisions needed (approval to hire/fire, approve price changes, capital spend)

Would you like me to:
- Build a simple 12‑month cash-flow scenario model with base / cost-reduction / growth cases using the assumptions above? (I’ll need monthly revenue, COGS, fixed costs, AR/AP days, inventory, any upcoming one-offs)
- Draft a one-page slide or an email/paragraph tailored to the Board or executive team with the numbers?
- Or proceed with the assumption that burn = net $240K and produce a concrete electricity-line item savings plan with expected $ impact?

Tell me which option and confirm how you define “burn.” If you prefer, paste your current monthly P&L summary (revenue, COGS, gross margin, fixed OPEX categories) and I’ll produce a modeled plan with exact dollar impacts.

