Thanks — I can help. First, one quick clarification so the numbers and narrative are internally consistent:

- You said: cash = $2.1M, burn = $240K/month, runway = 14 months. Those three don’t line up: 2.1M ÷ 240K ≈ 8.75 months runway. If your runway is actually 14 months, either cash or monthly burn figure is different (2.1M would imply monthly burn ≈ $150K to give 14 months). Which is correct: the burn figure ($240K) or the stated runway (14 months)? Also, is the $2.4M revenue “last quarter” recognized revenue (quarterly) or bookings/ARR? Any gross margin / COGS data and a recent P&L or breakdown of Opex would help.

Below I give a concise, actionable plan you can use in internal materials — margin-improvement levers, scalability levers, a recommended finance narrative, and a short scenario math section you can drop into slides.

1) Quick math (so you can pick the right framing)
- If burn = $240K/mo: runway = 2.1M / 240K = 8.8 months.
- If runway = 14 months is the true target, required burn = 2.1M / 14 ≈ $150K/mo. So you’d need to reduce monthly burn by ≈ $90K (≈37.5%) or offset the gap with additional near-term revenue or financing.
- To extend runway without headcount cuts, you can increase quarterly revenue or improve cash conversion (AR, inventory, vendor terms) — I’ll list specific levers below.

2) Top-line finance narrative (3–4 slides / talking points)
- Slide 1: Current position (facts + reconciliation)
  - Cash, stated runway (clarify), monthly net burn, revenue last quarter, gross margin (if known), key working-capital points.
  - One-sentence assessment: “At current burn, runway is X months; we must improve margins and/or reduce monthly cash outflow by $Y to achieve target runway of Z months.”
- Slide 2: Prioritized initiatives (impact + timing: quick wins 0–3 months, medium 3–9 months, long-term 9–18 months)
- Slide 3: Financial scenarios (Base / Downside / Upside) showing cash runway, EBITDA, break-even month, and top 3 KPIs to monitor
- Slide 4: Asks and next steps (approvals, hires, vendor negotiations, modelling)

3) Prioritized margin-improvement levers for a manufacturing company
(Quick wins first; estimate savings direction where possible)

A. Gross margin / COGS
- Supplier renegotiation and volume discounts: consolidate vendors, re-bid contracts. Potential: 2–8% of COGS depending on spend concentration.
- BOM and material substitution: lower-cost components or alternate sourcing—run design for cost reduction (DFX). Potential: meaningful per-unit savings on high-cost SKUs.
- Scrap/yield and process improvements: reduce waste in production (Lean/Six Sigma). Potential: 1–5% of production cost.
- SKU rationalization: stop low-margin/low-volume SKUs to improve blended margin.
- Pricing optimization: segment customers, raise price where elasticity allows, add value-based pricing for aftermarket/service. Small % increases in price can disproportionately help margins.

B. Operating expenses (Opex)
- Marketing & Sales: shift spend to highest-ROI channels; pause low-performing campaigns; lower CAC. Potential: 10–30% of marketing budget as quick savings.
- Headcount & contractors: hiring freeze, reduce contractors, or targeted reductions in non-core roles. Example: reducing payroll-linked costs by $X leads to $Y/month.
- G&A cuts: travel, subscriptions, consultants, office/real estate consolidation, renegotiate leases.
- Convert fixed to variable costs where possible (outsourced manufacturing, pay-for-performance vendors).

C. Working capital
- Shorten DSO (faster invoicing, incentive/discount for early pay) — converts receivables to cash.
- Reduce inventory (JIT, demand forecasting) to free cash.
- Extend payables where possible without harming supplier relationships.

4) Scalability levers (maintain/improve margins as you grow)
- Standardize and modularize products to reduce per-unit setup and engineering cost.
- Invest selectively in automation/ERP where it reduces variable labor cost per unit and improves throughput — model payback period (target < 18 months for critical automation).
- Channel scaling: move toward higher-margin channels (distributors with lower fixed SG&A burden, OEM partnerships, recurring service contracts).
- Productize services and spare parts for recurring revenue and higher margins.
- Sales enablement: reduce sales cycle/time-to-close, increase average deal size with bundles and upsells.
- Pricing tiers & value-added warranties or maintenance contracts to increase LTV and margins.

5) Sample prioritized action plan with rough timing & potential impact
- 0–30 days (quick wins)
  - Freeze hiring, prioritize essential projects. (Immediate savings)
  - Pause low-ROI marketing (10–30% of marketing spend).
  - Begin supplier renegotiations and request extended payment terms. (Cash improvement within 30–60 days)
  - Collect AR aggressively; offer small early-pay discounts. (Cash inflow)
- 30–90 days (operational & margin)
  - SKU rationalization & pricing clean-up: implement across channels.
  - Re-price top 20% of customers if data supports.
  - Convert contractors to part-time or eliminate low-impact roles.
- 3–9 months (medium-term)
  - Launch cost-reduction engineering projects (BOM changes).
  - Invest in targeted automation with modeled payback.
  - Build channel/enterprise sales motion for higher contract value.
- 9–18 months (long-term)
  - Scale production capacity, pursue vertical integration only where ROI > threshold.
  - Implement continuous improvement program.

6) Financial targets & KPIs to include in the narrative
- Runway (months) and net burn ($/month)
- Gross margin % and target improvement (e.g., +3–8 p.p. in 12 months)
- Contribution margin per product line
- Operating expense run rate and target reduction ($/month)
- DSO, DPO, Inventory turns
- Revenue growth rate (QoQ) and revenue mix shift to higher-margin products/services
- CAC, payback period, LTV if relevant to sales model

7) Scenario math example (slide-ready)
- Base (current): cash 2.1M; burn 240K → runway 8.8 months.
- Cost-reduction scenario: reduce burn by $60K/mo (25%) via hiring freeze, marketing cuts, vendor savings → new burn = 180K → runway = 11.7 months.
- Combined scenario: reduce burn by $45K + increase monthly net revenue by $45K (pricing, sales) → net burn 150K → runway = 14 months.
Use this to show trade-offs: you can get to 14 months by ~37.5% cost reduction OR by a mix of cost cuts + revenue gains.

8) What I can do next (pick one)
- I can draft a 4-slide internal deck using the narrative above (you can paste exact P&L figures and I’ll populate the slides).
- I can build a simple 12–18 month cash-flow scenario model (Base / Cost-cut / Growth) if you share: monthly revenue (or revenue last 6 months), gross margin %, COGS, fixed Opex (payroll, rent, marketing), AR days, inventory, and outstanding debt.
- I can write the exact oral script/text for the CFO/CEO to present to the exec team or board.

Tell me which you want and please confirm the correct burn and runway numbers (or provide the latest monthly P&L / top-line breakdown). If you prefer I start by drafting the slide text and the 3-scenario model using the numbers you gave, say “Draft slides + scenarios with current numbers” and I’ll proceed.

