Thanks — I can help. First, a quick fact-check and then a prioritized plan (short narrative you can use, concrete levers to improve margin & scalability, rough sensitivity math, KPIs to track, and recommended next steps).

1) Quick inconsistency check (please confirm)
- You gave: cash = $2.1M, burn = $240K/month, runway = 14 months, revenue last quarter = $2.4M.
- If burn = $240K/month then runway = 2.1M / 240K = 8.75 months (not 14). To have 14 months runway with $2.1M cash, net burn must be ~$150K/month (2.1M / 14 ≈ 150K).
Please confirm which of these three is authoritative (cash, burn, or runway) and whether “burn” is net burn (cash out − cash in) or operating cash burn before revenue offsets.

2) Executive finance narrative (short, internal-ready)
“Metro Manufacturing finished the quarter with $2.1M in cash and revenue of $2.4M for the quarter. Our immediate objective is to extend our runway while improving unit economics so we can scale sustainably. We will pursue a two-track approach this quarter: (1) margin improvement actions that lift gross contribution and reduce COGS, and (2) operating-leverage and scalability moves to lower net burn and enable faster growth. Target: move net burn from current levels to ≤$150K/month within 3–6 months (or extend runway to ~14 months) while improving gross margin by 5–10 percentage points through pricing, product mix and supplier changes. We’ll report progress weekly on contribution margin, CAC payback and runway impact.”

3) High-priority levers (what to do first — with expected impact / effort)
A. Short-term (0–90 days) — highest impact, low-to-medium effort
- Pricing & packaging: test a +3–7% price increase on key products or add value-based surcharges. Impact: straight revenue lift; margin uplift proportional to price change. Effort: low (A/B test).
- Supplier & COGS renegotiation: renegotiate volume discounts, alternative suppliers, or repack raw material contracts. Impact: 2–6 p.p. gross margin improvement. Effort: medium.
- Reduce discretionary OPEX / hiring freeze: pause non-essential hires and cut non-revenue software/licenses. Target: save 10–25% of SG&A. Impact: immediate burn reduction. Effort: low.
- Accounts & working capital: extend payables (where possible) and accelerate receivables or require deposits on large orders. Impact: immediate cash flow improvement. Effort: low.

B. Medium-term (1–6 months) — medium impact, medium effort
- Product mix optimization: push higher-margin SKUs, bundle services to increase AOV. Impact: materially raise contribution margin if >20% of sales can shift. Effort: medium.
- Automation & process efficiency: reduce manual labor in production or back office to lower variable costs. Impact: reduces unit operating cost; scalable. Effort: medium-high.
- Channel optimization & repeatable sales motions: focus on channels with best CAC and LTV and reduce spend on underperforming channels. Impact: lowers CAC, improves unit economics. Effort: medium.

C. Strategic (6–18 months) — higher effort, high impact
- Platformize services / increase recurring revenue: shift one-off sales to subscription/consumables for predictable top-line and better LTV. Impact: stabilizes revenue and reduces net burn volatility. Effort: high.
- Strategic partnerships / co-manufacturing: share fixed costs and raise utilization. Impact: increases scale with lower incremental op-ex cost. Effort: high.

4) Sensitivity examples (useful for slides)
Assumptions: last-quarter revenue = $2.4M → monthly revenue ≈ $800K.

Runway math examples (confirm whether burn is net burn):
- If net burn = $240K → runway = 2.1M / 240K = 8.75 months.
- To achieve 14 months runway with $2.1M cash → required net burn = 2.1M / 14 ≈ $150K → reduction needed = $90K/month.
Impact of specific moves (illustrative):
- Improve gross margin by 5 percentage points: incremental monthly cash = 800K × 0.05 = $40K → net burn falls to ≈ $200K → runway = 2.1M / 200K = 10.5 months.
- Cut SG&A/hiring by 20% of current monthly SG&A (assuming SG&A equals 240K burn component): if you save $50K/month → net burn falls to 190K → runway ≈ 11.05 months.
- Combine 5ppt margin lift ($40K) + $50K cost cuts → net burn down to 150K → runway ≈ 14 months. (So a moderate mix of margin improvements + SG&A cuts gets you to your 14-month target.)

5) KPIs to present and track weekly/monthly
- Net burn ($/month) and runway (months)
- Gross margin % and gross profit $ (monthly)
- Contribution margin per unit / per customer
- Revenue growth (% QoQ), ARR or recurring rev %
- CAC, CAC payback months, LTV, LTV:CAC
- Customer concentration (top 5 customers %)
- Working capital days (DSO, DPO, inventory turns)

6) Suggested 1-page slide structure (internal)
- Top line: cash, net burn, runway (clear numbers and math), last quarter revenue
- One-line objective (e.g., “Reach ≤$150K/mo net burn; +5–10ppt gross margin in 3 months”)
- Quick actions (3 short-term + 3 medium-term) with expected $/month or ppt impact
- Sensitivity table: baseline, +margin lift, +cost cuts, combined (show runway)
- Ask or decisions required (approve hiring freeze, authorize supplier negotiations, pilot price increase)

7) Quick canned text you can drop in an email to leadership
“Summary: With $2.1M in cash and $2.4M revenue last quarter, our priority is to extend runway and improve contribution margin so growth scales without excessive cash consumption. Recommended immediate actions: (1) test a targeted price increase, (2) renegotiate supplier terms, (3) implement a temporary hiring freeze and reduce discretionary SG&A. These moves should together generate $80–120K/month of cash improvement and get us toward a ~14-month runway target. I’ll build a 3-scenario model (base / cost-savings / growth) once you confirm whether the $240K burn is net and share current gross margin and SG&A split.”

8) Next steps — what I need from you to build the actual model and slides
- Confirm which number is authoritative: net burn = $240K or runway = 14 months? Is burn net of revenue?
- Current gross margin % (or COGS $) and monthly SG&A break-down
- Recurring vs one-time revenue %, customer concentration, any planned hires or capital expenditures
- Audience for the narrative (board, exec team, investors) and timeline for the presentation

If you confirm those items I’ll:
- Build a 3-scenario financial model (monthly P&L / runway) showing exactly how each lever impacts burn and runway
- Produce a one-page slide + 1-paragraph executive narrative you can use for leadership or the board

