High-level finance narrative (one paragraph you can use)
“Metro Manufacturing currently generates ~$800K/month in revenue and holds $2.1M in cash. We face a finite runway that requires immediate margin and working capital action. Our plan focuses on three pillars: (1) quick cash preservation (90-day actions to extend runway), (2) gross margin improvement through pricing, product mix, and procurement, and (3) scalable operating design (automation, process standardization, and expanded higher-margin channels). We quantify each lever and prioritize those that deliver the greatest extension to runway and fastest ROI. We will track weekly cash, contribution margin by product, and customer profitability to ensure progress.”

Priority levers — quick wins (0–90 days)
- Immediate pricing review: increase prices on laggard or bespoke discount accounts where value is clear; run tiered tests.
- Stop-gap OPEX controls: hiring freeze, pause non-essential projects, freeze variable spend (travel, conferences).
- Working capital fixes: extend payables where possible, accelerate receivables (early-pay discounts, tighten terms), reduce excess inventory.
- Supplier short-term renegotiation: volume discounts, longer payment terms, or temporary price relief.
- Product rationalization: temporarily stop producing/fulfilling lowest-margin SKUs.

Priority levers — medium term (3–12 months)
- Secure better BOM cost via supplier consolidation or competitive bids; redesign for lower cost-per-unit.
- Move customers to higher-margin offerings, add service/maintenance contracts or recurring revenue elements.
- Operational scaling: invest in automation/ERP to reduce variable labor per unit and increase throughput.
- Channel optimization: shift sales to higher-margin channels (direct vs low-margin distributors) and implement value-based pricing.
- Lean manufacturing & continuous improvement to raise yields and reduce scrap.

Scalability levers (structural)
- Unit economics playbook: standardize contribution margin per SKU/customer; only scale accounts/products that meet a minimum CM threshold.
- Repeatable production cells / modular product architecture to reduce incremental cost of volume.
- Sales playbook and enablement to reduce CAC and shorten sales cycles.
- Platform services (maintenance, software, consumables) to increase customer LTV and recurring revenue.

KPIs to track (weekly / monthly)
- Gross margin % (overall and by SKU/customer)
- Contribution margin per unit and product family
- Monthly net burn (cash outflows minus cash inflows)
- Cash runway (weeks/months)
- DSO / DPO / inventory turns (working capital)
- Customer concentration (top 5 customers % of revenue)
- CAC, LTV, payback period (if relevant)
- Overtime/temporary labor as % of production cost

Worked example — illustrative impact (pick whichever burn number is correct)
Assumptions:
- Revenue = $800K / month
- Scenario A baseline gross margin = 30% → gross profit = $240K/mo
- Two interpretations of current net burn:
  - If runway = 14 months: implied net burn = 2.1M / 14 ≈ $150K/mo → implies OPEX ≈ $390K/mo (because 390K − 240K = 150K)
  - If burn = $240K/mo (your figure): runway ≈ 2.1M / 240K ≈ 8.8 months → implies OPEX ≈ $480K/mo (480K − 240K = 240K)

Illustrative lever impacts (numbers are examples to show scale):
- Improve gross margin by +5 percentage points (30% → 35%): monthly gross profit increases from $240K → $280K (+$40K).
- Reduce OPEX by 10% (if OPEX = $390K → $351K): monthly OPEX saves $39K.
- Combined effect (GM +5pp and OPEX −10%): net burn improvement = +$79K/month.

Using the 14-month baseline (net burn $150K):
- Base runway = 14 months.
- With GM +5pp only → new net burn = 150K − 40K = 110K → new runway = 2.1M / 110K ≈ 19.1 months (≈ +5 months).
- With OPEX −10% only → new net burn = 150K − 39K = 111K → runway ≈ 18.9 months (≈ +5 months).
- Combined → new net burn ≈ 71K → runway ≈ 29.6 months (≈ +15 months).

Using the 240K burn baseline:
- Base runway ≈ 8.8 months.
- GM +5pp → burn = 240K − 40K = 200K → runway = 2.1M / 200K = 10.5 months.
- OPEX −10% (on 480K) → saves 48K → new burn = 240K − 48K = 192K → runway = 11.0 months.
- Combined → new burn = 240K − 88K = 152K → runway = 13.8 months.

Key takeaway from these examples: relatively modest gross margin improvements (a few percentage points) and modest OPEX reduction materially extend runway. Combined small, realistic wins are multiplicative.

