- Cut discretionary spend (travel, marketing pilots, subscriptions) — impact: $5k–$20k/mo.
- Accounts receivable acceleration (shorter terms, early pay discounts) — one‑time cash boost; improves working capital.
- Stretch payables (while preserving supplier relationships) — preserves cash short-term.
Expected near-term impact: 20k–70k/month.

High-impact operational levers (60–180 days)
- Vendor renegotiation & consolidated purchasing: renegotiate raw material and freight contracts, volume discounts — impact: $20k–$60k/mo.
- Manufacturing efficiency: OEE improvement, reduce scrap and rework, reduce overtime — impact: $20k–80k/mo depending on current inefficiencies.
- SKU rationalization: drop low-margin SKUs to improve average gross margin — impact: increases gross margin %; could add $10k–$50k/mo to gross profit.
Expected medium-term impact: 30k–120k/mo.

Revenue / pricing levers (60–180 days)
- Targeted price increases (value-based pricing, small % increases across the portfolio) — a 2–5% price increase on $800k = $16k–$40k/mo.
- Upsell/cross-sell to existing customers, introduce higher‑margin service offerings (maintenance, warranty, premium support) — recurring margin improvements.
- Channel optimization: shift to direct or more profitable distributors, increase customer lifetime value.
Expected impact: 20k–100k/mo.

Structural scalability (3–12 months)
- Shift cost structure from fixed → variable (outsourcing, contract manufacturing for low-volume SKUs).
- Invest in automation/CapEx selectively where payback < 18 months to lower long-term COGS.
- Product platform rationalization to reduce complexity and scale production efficiently.
